    <DataNode elementId="STAUROSPORINE" textLabel="staurosporine" type="Metabolite">
      <Xref identifier="HKSZLNNOFSGOKW-FYTWVXJKSA-O" dataSource="InChIKey" />
      <Graphics centerX="500" centerY="400" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="3955e7" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="3955e7" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="PMN, zhang, SRI, caspi">|FRAME: STAUROSPORINE &quot;Staurosporine&quot;| was isolated in 1977 (under the name AM-2282) from a culture of |FRAME: TAX-65499 &quot;&lt;i&gt;Lentzea albida&lt;/i&gt; (then known as &lt;i&gt;Streptomyces stauroporeus&lt;/i&gt; AM-2282)&quot;| that was collected from a soil sample in Japan |CITS: [863788]|. Staurosporine and related natural indolocarbazole compounds have been isolated from actinomycetes, myxomycetes (slime molds) and cyanobacteria. They have also been found in some marine invertebrates such as sponges, tunicates, bryozoans and mollusks, where they may be synthesized by symbiotic bacteria |CITS: [17119643]|. For a list of strains shown to produce indolocarbazole compounds see |CITS: [17119643]|. In 1986 it was shown to be nanomolar inhibitor of protein kinases |CITS: [3457562]|. During the 1990s protein kinases became the second most important drug target after G-protein-coupled receptors, and |FRAME: STAUROSPORINE| became the focus of much research. |FRAME: CPD-11911| (UCN-01) showed antitumor activity in mouse tumor models and entered clinical studies |CITS: [1893379]|. Staurosporine-related structures have been derivatized and developed into selective inhibitors of pharmacologically interesting targets |CITS: [19132059]|.</Comment>
      <Property key="UniqueID" value="STAUROSPORINE" />
